 to say that's really saying

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.400
 pull in the regular expression library.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.200
 And there is a couple of functions inside that.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.000
 re.search, which is kind of like a really smart version

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.520
 of the find method inside of strings,

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:08.160
 and re.findall, which is kind of like

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.520
 taking and stamping your way through a loop,

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.340
 through a string, and finding all of the things

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:17.400
 that match a particular pattern and then extracting those.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.560
 And we'll talk about both of these in this lecture.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.040
 So here's a really simple piece of code

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:25.480
 where I'm just gonna sort of show you

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:27.080
 sort of before and after.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.960
 So here's a thing where we're looking for lines

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.440
 that begin with from colon.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.360
 And so we open a file, we loop through the whole file,

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.360
 we strip off the lines text,

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.080
 and then we say if line.find from

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:41.640
 is greater than or equal to zero, then we print it.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.200
 It gives you negative one if it's not found,

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.760
 and so reads all the lines,

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:45.760
 and once in a while it'll print it out,

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:47.520
 and reads all the lines once in a while it'll print it out.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.320
 So that's kind of like a needle in the haystack.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.760
 To use regular expressions to do that,

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.120
 we have to import the regular expression library.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:55.760
 These lines are the same, we're gonna loop through,

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:59.540
 we're gonna strip, and now we're gonna say if re.search.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:02.440
 The way to say this is within the library regular expressions,

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:04.760
 go find the search function,

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:09.720
 and search for the string from in the string line, okay?

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:10.880
 So this is the line to search,

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:12.920
 whereas here it was more object-oriented

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.320
 where we say line.find.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.200
 We say re.search and we pass in line as parameter.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:18.280
 These two things are equivalent,

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:19.560
 which means most of the time it's gonna run,

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:20.520
 and once in a while it'll hit a line,

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:21.880
 and it'll print that out,

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:23.440
 and then it'll finish the whole thing.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.040
 So that is taking and doing what we would do

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.360
 with the find operation with regular expressions.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.360
 Now, searching with regular expressions

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:35.640
 has these special characters,

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.880
 and so here we have the same basic code,

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:40.320
 except now we're saying if line starts with from.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:41.920
 So we're not using find anymore,

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.440
 and that way we're only gonna get that thing

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:46.520
 in the first position,

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:50.000
 not like blah, blah, blah, blah from colon.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:50.960
 We don't want that to match.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.240
 We only want it to match here at the beginning of the line,

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:54.760
 and so that's what we use line starts with.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:55.920
 So it's gonna do the same thing

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.560
 and find lines that have the prefix

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.880
 and print those out and then be done.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.040
 Now, in regular expression search,

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:03.360
 we don't, in a sense, change the method.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:05.800
 We have a certain number of things we can do with strings

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:07.360
 based on what they've built in,

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.600
 but in regular expression we actually can turn

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.480
 this first parameter into code,

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:16.080
 and so what's happening here is the caret,

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:17.480
 if you go back to the little cheat sheet,

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:20.120
 caret means this is the beginning of line.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.200
 It's a virtual character that matches the beginning line.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.160
 It's like from that starts at the beginning,

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.120
 so from at the beginning does match,

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:28.800
 and from in the middle does not match

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.560
 by putting that little caret there.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:32.520
 Same thing, line is what we're searching,

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:34.480
 and then from is what we caret from,

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.480
 line at the beginning is what we're looking for,

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:39.480
 and so again it does the exact same thing,

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:41.080
 and only prints lines that have from colon

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:43.320
 as the first character in the line.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.840
 So the difference is is we look for a method,

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:48.720
 and the other one is we program the regular expression.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.840
 So we're gonna run out of methods in the string class

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.400
 long before we run out of things

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.740
 that we can do with regular expressions.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:00.040
 And so a couple other special characters

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:03.000
 that caret matches the beginning of the line,

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.960
 so caret matches the beginning of the line.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:07.440
 This capital X matches itself.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.360
 Dot is a wild card that matches any character,

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.800
 and then some of the characters in regular expressions

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:15.080
 modify the immediately preceding character,

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:18.720
 and so that says look for a line that starts with X

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:22.160
 and then has many characters, that's these two things,

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:25.200
 zero or more characters followed by a colon.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:26.980
 And so you can see that it's sort of,

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:29.000
 it's this sort of like expanding stamp.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:30.920
 It's like oh, there's an X at the beginning of that line.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:31.860
 It looks good.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.480
 I got some characters here, and then I got a colon.

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:38.040
 That's good, so this is an X, some characters, and a colon.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:41.160
 Check, X, some characters, and a colon.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.040
 Check, X, and these things, you know, away we go.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:46.160
 And so you can, that's what's gonna match.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.760
 And so you can see how some of these characters are special.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:50.320
 Again, go back to your cheat sheet.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:51.320
 Some of them are special,

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:52.960
 and some of them are actual characters.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.640
 And this colon and X are just, they're not special.

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:58.520
 They're just the characters, okay?

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:02.280
 Now, sometimes you wanna be a little more clear

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:05.000
 on your match, so let's take a look at these lines

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.120
 that match that particular thing that we just did.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:11.040
 So we have these two, X dash sieve colon,

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:14.360
 X dash destam dash result, like these are from mail messages.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.160
 And then one of the mail messages has a line in it

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.120
 that says X dash plain is behind schedule.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:21.240
 And this matches.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:22.880
 Is that what you really wanted?

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:26.640
 And so what we can basically say is because this is an X,

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:29.560
 this is some number of characters, and that's a colon,

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:30.800
 it matches, it has to match.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:34.960
 That's this rule applied to this line results in a yes.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:35.920
 It does.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:39.400
 And so how can you be a little more clear

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:40.800
 as to what you want to match

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:42.800
 and what you don't want to match?

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.720
 So we can write code.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.480
 So now what we're going to say is

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:51.720
 we wanna match the beginning of the line,

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.440
 and we wanna capital X, and we wanna dash.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:56.320
 So now we're gonna match those first two characters,

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:58.760
 X dash, at the beginning of the line.

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:00.820
 Carat X dash says first two characters of the line

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:02.060
 must be X dash.

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:03.560
 Now we have another special character.

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.440
 Now again, refer to your cheat sheet.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.600
 Backslash capital S means a non-white space character.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:12.700
 Any character other than white space.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:15.100
 And then plus means one or more times,

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.500
 one or more non-white space characters.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:19.320
 That's what this whole thing says.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.280
 One or more non-white space characters,

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.280
 and followed by a colon, which is just a character.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:25.640
 So now we have X dash followed by one or more

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:27.960
 non-white space characters followed by a colon.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.360
 X dash followed by one or more non-white space characters

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:31.560
 followed by a colon.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:33.680
 And here we have X dash followed by one or more,

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:35.480
 whoops, there's a space there.

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:36.800
 And so this doesn't match.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:38.240
 Even though there's a colon there,

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.680
 it means that between the dash and the colon,

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:43.660
 you can only have some number of non-white space characters.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:47.160
 So this is a no, it does not match.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:50.520
 And so you just can, if you didn't wanna match this,

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:53.760
 you then sort of create a more precise,

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.800
 you know, we could even have a thing that said

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:59.560
 I want X dash with an uppercase character,

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:01.360
 uppercase letter, if you wanted to.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.120
 So there's all kind of fine-tuning

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.520
 if you sort of learn the structure that you've got to do.

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.360
 And so that's kind of the matching

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:10.760
 where you're taking a whole line

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:12.680
 and taking this template and deciding

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:15.440
 if the template anywhere in that line matches.

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:17.000
 And now what we're gonna do is use this

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:20.220
 to actually pull data out of strings

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:22.120
 using the regular expression library.

